 # Regularization and Loss Functions
 
-Also called: Augmented Loss, Regularization Terms, Auxiliary Losses
 
 ## Overview
-Regularization and Loss Functions extend the standard photometric reconstruction loss ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{photo}}$) with auxiliary terms ($\mathcal{L}_k$) to constrain the optimization of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), particularly in scenarios with sparse views, ambiguous geometry, or generative synthesis [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]. The total loss is defined as $\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{photo}} + \sum_k \lambda_k \mathcal{L}_k$ [S1, 10636-specific-loss-formulations.md]. This family sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the *objective function* rather than the sampling strategy, and from [Training Configuration](2332-training-configuration.md) by defining the specific mathematical constraints rather than hyperparameter schedules.
+Regularization and Loss Functions define the objective landscape for optimizing Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) and related differentiable volume rendering models. While standard photometric reconstruction minimizes pixel-wise color differences, these methods introduce additional constraints—such as geometric smoothness, semantic consistency, or generative priors—to resolve ambiguities inherent in sparse-view or ill-posed reconstruction tasks [S1, 7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md, 8582-regnerf.md]. They set themselves apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the *what* to optimize (the loss landscape) rather than the *how* of sampling, and from [Training Configuration](2332-training-configuration.md) by defining the mathematical terms rather than the hyperparameter schedules.
 
