 ### [Diffusion-regularized NeRF](7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md)
-Diffusion-regularized NeRF improves geometry and color reconstruction in limited-view scenarios by incorporating a Denoising Diffusion Model (DDM) as a learned prior. It backpropagates the gradient of the log-probability of RGBD patches estimated by the DDM directly to the NeRF's density and color fields. It distinguishes itself from [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md) by regularizing geometry directly via RGBD patches rather than indirectly via RGB patches, and from [Depth and Geometry Supervision](8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md) by using a generative diffusion prior rather than explicit geometric constraints.
+Diffusion-regularized NeRF improves geometric plausibility in limited-view scenarios by incorporating a Denoising Diffusion Model (DDM) as a learned prior. It differs from sibling methods by backpropagating the score function of RGBD patches learned from synthetic data, addressing artifacts like "floaters". Choose this when training on few views and needing strong geometric priors without explicit depth labels.
 
 ### [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md)
-RegNeRF synthesizes novel views from sparse input images by applying depth smoothness and color priors as regularization terms during training. It solves the problem of NeRF training with sparse input views without requiring scene-specific pretraining. Unlike sibling methods that rely on pretrained feature extractors or adversarial training, RegNeRF constrains unobserved views using a Normalizing Flow prior for color and a geometric smoothness prior for depth, achieving performance comparable to pretrained models on sparse datasets.
+RegNeRF synthesizes novel views from sparse inputs by applying depth smoothness and color priors (Normalizing Flows) to unobserved regions. It differs from [DiffNeRF](8572-diffnerf.md) by using finite-difference approximations on patches rather than automatic differentiation of the ray representation, and from [Depth and Geometry Supervision](8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md) by not requiring pretraining on the target dataset. Choose this for sparse-view reconstruction where generative priors are beneficial.
 
 ### [Depth and Geometry Supervision](8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md)
-Depth and Geometry Supervision incorporates external geometric information—such as sparse 3D keypoints, monocular depth priors, or multi-view consistency constraints—into NeRF training. These methods solve the problem of geometric ambiguity and overfitting to incorrect surfaces, which is prevalent in sparse-view settings or texture-less regions where photometric loss alone is insufficient. Unlike sibling methods that rely on semantic consistency [11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md] or diffusion priors [7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md], this family explicitly aligns the rendered ray termination distributions or depth expectations with ground-truth or estimated geometric structures.
+These methods enhance optimization by incorporating explicit geometric constraints (depth, points, or structural priors) into the training loop. They distinguish themselves from [Diffusion-regularized NeRF](7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md) by relying on explicit geometric signals rather than generative image priors, and from [Volumetric Supervision](11100-volumetric-supervision.md) by focusing on ray termination distribution alignment. Choose this when external geometric data (depth maps, point clouds) is available.
 
 ### [Specific Loss Formulations](10636-specific-loss-formulations.md)
-This family comprises specific mathematical objectives and weighting strategies used to regularize or guide NeRF optimization beyond standard photometric reconstruction. It details the concrete formulations of $\mathcal{L}_k$ terms that address specific failure modes such as misalignment, distributed computation artifacts, frequency mismatches, and domain-specific constraints like compression or generative consistency [10636-specific-loss-formulations.md]. These methods set themselves apart by introducing specialized metrics (e.g., perceptual, distortion, or GAN-based losses) or computational mechanisms (e.g., cross-GPU aggregation) not covered by generic geometric or semantic regularizers [10636-specific-loss-formulations.md].
+This family comprises specific objective functions targeting distinct failure modes such as photometric error, geometric distortion, and high-frequency detail loss. They differ from [Diffusion-regularized NeRF](7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md) by focusing on pixel-level, frequency-domain, or distributional statistics rather than semantic or generative priors. Choose this for fine-tuning specific aspects of reconstruction quality.
 
 ### [Multiplex Training via S3IM](10894-multiplex-training-via-s3im.md)
-Multiplex Training via S3IM combines a conventional pixel-wise loss (e.g., MSE or L1) with a Stochastic Structural SIMilarity (S3IM) loss to improve neural field reconstruction quality, particularly for complex details and sparse inputs. It sets itself apart from standard SSIM optimization by using stochastic patch sampling to capture non-local structural information from distant pixels across different images, whereas standard SSIM via local patches only captures structural information from nearby pixels within a single image.
+This paradigm combines conventional pixel-wise loss with Stochastic Structural SIMilarity (S3IM) to improve reconstruction of complex details. It sets itself apart from standard SSIM by using stochastic patch generation to capture non-local structural information from distant pixels across different images, whereas standard SSIM only captures local structure. Choose this when standard photometric losses fail to capture high-frequency structural coherence.
 
 ### [S3IM (Stochastic Structural SIMilarity)](10895-s3im-stochastic-structural-similarity.md)
-S3IM is a multiplex regularization term for neural fields that captures nonlocal structural similarity by processing multiple data points (pixels/rays) as a whole set, rather than individually. It addresses the limitation of point-wise losses (like MSE) which fail to utilize the collective supervision of distant pixels. Unlike conventional MSE loss, S3IM cannot be expressed as the sum of any other loss computed over individual pixels from the batch.
+S3IM is a nonlocal multiplex loss that captures structural similarity by processing multiple data points as a whole set, rather than individually. Unlike conventional MSE, S3IM cannot be expressed as the sum of any other loss computed over individual pixels from the batch. Choose this for neural fields where global structural consistency is more important than local pixel accuracy.
 
 ### [Volumetric Supervision](11100-volumetric-supervision.md)
-Volumetric Supervision leverages high-quality volumetric reconstructions from conventional tomography to supervise neural network training at intermediate time steps where only sparse projection data is available. It solves the problem of reconstructing dynamic volumetric scenes from sparse angular views by using dense ground-truth volumes from the start and end of the experiment as anchors. Unlike sibling methods that rely on photometric, geometric, or semantic losses, this method directly minimizes the discrepancy between the predicted 3D volume and a reference 3D volume using a normalized correlation coefficient.
+This strategy leverages high-quality volumetric reconstructions from conventional tomography to supervise neural network training at intermediate time steps. Unlike sibling methods that enforce geometric consistency via depth or gradient losses, this method directly minimizes the discrepancy between the predicted 3D volume and a physically reconstructed 3D volume. Choose this for dynamic tomography or temporal interpolation tasks with sparse projection data.
 
 ### [Style Loss](11193-style-loss.md)
-Style Loss is a regularization term used in differentiable volume rendering to enforce color and style coherence between a generated object (or scene region) and a reference image. It operates by minimizing the distance between feature representations in a pre-trained VGG feature space. Unlike [Perceptual Loss](13426-perceptual-loss.md), which minimizes the distance to a fixed reference feature vector, Style Loss captures statistical texture statistics (global) and local contextual similarities (local) to constrain the generated content's appearance. It is distinct from [Nearest Neighbor Feature Matching Loss](13527-nearest-neighbor-feature-matching-loss.md) in that it explicitly combines global Gram-matrix-like statistics with a contextual nearest-neighbor search, whereas NNFM focuses solely on the latter to preserve high-frequency details like brushstrokes.
+Style Loss enforces color and style coherence between a generated object and a reference image by minimizing the distance between feature representations in a pre-trained VGG feature space. Unlike [Perceptual Loss](13426-perceptual-loss.md), it incorporates a local contextual component that searches for the closest matching feature, allowing for more flexible texture and style transfer. Choose this for object insertion or style transfer tasks in NeRFs.
 
 ### [Confidence-Based Loss Calibration](11347-confidence-based-loss-calibration.md)
-Confidence-Based Loss Calibration (ICON) solves the problem of joint optimization instability in NeRF and camera pose estimation by dynamically weighting gradients based on the reliability of the current solution state. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [RegNeRF](8582-regnerf.md) or [Depth and Geometry Supervision](8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md) by explicitly decoupling the confidence of the radiance field from the confidence of the camera poses, using these metrics to modulate the respective gradient updates rather than applying static regularization terms.
+This method dynamically weights optimization using estimated reliability metrics, addressing bas-relief ambiguity and registration failures. It sets itself apart from static regularization by using pose confidence to weight NeRF gradients and ray confidence to weight pose gradients, effectively decoupling error sources. Choose this for joint optimization of NeRF and camera poses where registration quality varies.
 
 ### [Semantic Consistency Loss (CLIP-based)](11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md)
-Semantic Consistency Loss (SCL) is a regularization term that enforces semantic similarity between rendered images and ground-truth training images (or between unseen views) using embeddings from a pre-trained CLIP-ViT network. It solves the problem of photometric ambiguity in NeRF training by ensuring that the rendered scene preserves high-level semantic structure, making it robust to pixel-wise misalignment. It sets itself apart from photometric losses by operating in the embedding space rather than pixel space, and from direct semantic supervision methods by using self-supervised 2D features as a teacher signal without requiring 3D semantic labels.
+Semantic Consistency Loss (SCL) enforces semantic coherence using features from the CLIP-ViT model. It solves photometric ambiguity in sparse-view scenes by providing a robust, high-level semantic prior invariant to pixel-wise misalignment. It differs from [Diffusion-regularized NeRF](7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md) by leveraging pre-trained vision-language alignment rather than generative priors. Choose this for text-guided generation or sparse-view reconstruction where semantic structure is critical.
 
